cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Energy System Modeling: companion code"
message: "If you use this material, please cite it. To cite the textbook rather than the code, cite the book (see references below)."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Jones
    given-names: Erick C.
    name-suffix: Jr.
    affiliation: University of Texas at Arlington
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0559-4699"
repository-code: "https://github.com/sear-labs/energy-system-modeling"
abstract: >-
  Teaching notebooks and a Python package accompanying the open textbook
  Introduction to Energy System Modeling. Each notebook builds an energy-system
  model by hand and checks it against the package, covering energy balances,
  representative days, end-use disaggregation, capital cost and LCOE, pipeline
  transport, DC power flow and locational marginal pricing, real network import,
  material requirements, and a multi-city Texas capacity buildout.
keywords:
  - energy system modeling
  - optimization
  - PyPSA
  - linear programming
  - power systems
  - teaching
  - jupyter notebooks
license: MIT
license-url: "https://github.com/sear-labs/energy-system-modeling/blob/main/LICENSE-DATA"
references:
  - type: book
    title: "Introduction to Energy System Modeling"
    authors:
      - family-names: Jones
        given-names: Erick C.
        name-suffix: Jr.
    publisher:
      name: "University of Texas at Arlington Libraries (Pressbooks)"
    url: "https://uta.pressbooks.pub/energysystemmodeling"
    year: 2026
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NO doi: FIELD YET, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
#
# Zenodo mints two DOIs against the first tagged release: a version DOI, new
# every release, and a concept DOI, which always resolves to the latest version
# and never changes. The CONCEPT DOI belongs here, and the order is forced --
# you cannot have a DOI before the first release.
#
#   1. this file, with no doi                                     <- we are here
#   2. connect the repo in Zenodo and tag a release. SET THE
#      LICENCE ON THE DEPOSIT EXPLICITLY: the field is required
#      and defaults to CC-BY, which is wrong for MIT code
#   3. Zenodo mints both DOIs
#   4. add the concept DOI here and commit
#   5. never touch it again
#
# `license: MIT` above covers the code. The prose, notebooks and figures are
# CC BY 4.0 and the authored tables are CC0 -- CFF takes one licence field, so
# license-url points at LICENSE-DATA, which states all of them by path.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
